1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,600
Proposition forty-one combines audits with changes to state spending-limit

2
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:07,960
rules.

3
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:11,120
A special tax dedicates its money to a particular

4
00:00:11,120 --> 00:00:14,060
purpose, unlike revenue that can be used generally.

5
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:17,880
The measure concerns new or increased state special taxes.

6
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:21,920
California already has an independent state auditor and a

7
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:23,600
constitutional spending limit.

8
00:00:24,120 --> 00:00:27,940
This proposal adds reviews at particular stages and could

9
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:31,640
restrict the ability to exempt new special-tax spending from

10
00:00:31,640 --> 00:00:32,160
that limit.

11
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:36,180
Imagine a program manager proposing a dedicated funding measure.

12
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:40,440
Suppose a citizen initiative proposes a new tax for

13
00:00:40,440 --> 00:00:41,220
a public program.

14
00:00:41,820 --> 00:00:45,260
After supporters collect a quarter of the required signatures,

15
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:49,080
the auditor would review the program, even though the

16
00:00:49,080 --> 00:00:50,800
initiative might never qualify.

17
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:54,340
An auditor examines how the program uses its money.

18
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:57,980
If the initiative reaches the ballot, voters receive an

19
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:00,500
audit summary in the voter guide.

20
00:01:01,220 --> 00:01:04,520
That could provide useful information, but producing the review

21
00:01:04,520 --> 00:01:06,300
still takes staff and money.

22
00:01:07,100 --> 00:01:10,040
The initial audit must identify ways to reduce annual

23
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:11,440
program costs by ten percent.

24
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:15,640
Identifying options is not the same as delivering savings

25
00:01:15,940 --> 00:01:18,840
or proving that services can stay unchanged.

26
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:22,740
Programs funded by new or increased special taxes approved

27
00:01:22,740 --> 00:01:27,140
after January first, twenty twenty-six, would also face recurring

28
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:28,940
audits every four years.

29
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:34,260
Approved taxes generally fund the audits; unsuccessful initiatives can

30
00:01:34,260 --> 00:01:36,160
leave costs to the General Fund.

31
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:39,480
The analyst expects audit costs in the low millions

32
00:01:39,480 --> 00:01:43,580
annually, growing over time, plus voter-guide expenses.

33
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:48,440
Savings depend on decisions to implement effective recommendations, so

34
00:01:48,440 --> 00:01:50,320
the overall fiscal result is unknown.

35
00:01:50,320 --> 00:01:53,820
The spending-limit provisions are a separate issue.

36
00:01:54,440 --> 00:01:57,440
Prop forty-one could block Prop forty if both pass

37
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:01,820
and forty-one receives more yes votes, subject to how

38
00:02:01,820 --> 00:02:03,560
courts interpret their conflict.

39
00:02:04,080 --> 00:02:07,180
A voter might want evidence of efficiency before approving

40
00:02:07,180 --> 00:02:10,740
new dedicated taxes, and regular follow-up afterward.

41
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:14,320
They may also prefer keeping new revenue within spending

42
00:02:14,320 --> 00:02:14,700
constraints.

43
00:02:15,640 --> 00:02:19,360
Those are two distinct reasons to consider, rather than

44
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:21,300
assuming audits are the entire measure.

45
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:24,960
You might ask whether extra reviews justify their cost.

46
00:02:25,540 --> 00:02:29,500
Another voter could favor oversight but object to mandatory

47
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:33,160
reviews of measures that never qualify, or to limits

48
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:34,720
on funding flexibility.

49
00:02:35,520 --> 00:02:37,800
They may also oppose the effect on Prop forty.

50
00:02:38,820 --> 00:02:41,840
An audit requirement does not automatically mean waste is

51
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:42,260
eliminated.

52
00:02:43,620 --> 00:02:46,700
In the September PPIC survey of likely voters, 51

53
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:50,580
percent said yes, 44 percent no, and 5 percent

54
00:02:50,580 --> 00:02:51,100
were unsure.

55
00:02:51,940 --> 00:02:55,280
These are dated survey results, not votes already cast.

56
00:02:56,080 --> 00:02:59,740
A later September Berkeley survey found 37 percent yes,

57
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:02,580
33 percent no, and 30 percent undecided.

58
00:03:03,680 --> 00:03:07,020
Question wording and methods differ, so comparing polls does

59
00:03:07,020 --> 00:03:09,340
not by itself establish a trend.

60
00:03:10,260 --> 00:03:14,560
Yes adds audits and spending-limit provisions; no keeps the

61
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:15,380
current framework.

62
00:03:16,280 --> 00:03:19,220
Visit our website for the fuller explanation, source links,

63
00:03:19,220 --> 00:03:20,740
and official voter guide.

64
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:24,760
Compare the tradeoffs, then make your own informed choice.
